However, this doesn't seem to work as expected. The `variant` prop doesn't seem to have any effect on the component's styling.

To make this work with Tailwind CSS, you'll need to use the `@apply` directive to apply Tailwind classes instead of using styled-components. Here's how you can do it:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { Container, Row, Col } from 'reactstrap';

// Import Tailwind CSS directives
import 'tailwindcss/tailwind.css';
import 'tailwindcss/utils/keyframes';
import 'tailwindcss/components';
import 'tailwindcss/responsive';

const MyLayout = ({ children, variant }) => {
  return (
    <div className={`w-full ${variant === 'centered' ? 'flex justify-center items-center' : ''}`}>
      <Container>
        <Row>
          <Col>
            {children}
          </Col>
        </Row>
      </Container>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyLayout;
```

In this example, the `w-full` class is applied to the container to make it full-width, and the `flex justify-center items-center` classes are applied when the `variant` prop is 'centered'. This way, you're using Tailwind CSS classes directly in your component.

